    ],
    "reading_order": 12,
    "text": "Some of you might have come up with a formula predicting the number of regions\ngiven the number of points. From Class IX, you may remember that this intelligent\nguess is called a ‘conjecture’.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH1A1",
    "page": 7,
    "block_id": "jemh1a1_p007_b13",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      72.0,
      316.76,
      420.0,
      329.01
    ],
    "reading_order": 13,
    "text": "Suppose your conjecture is that given ‘n’ points on a circle, there are 2n – 1",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH1A1",
    "page": 7,
    "block_id": "jemh1a1_p007_b14",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      329.73,
      420.09,
      485.26
    ],
    "reading_order": 14,
    "text": "mutually exclusive regions, created by joining the points with all possible lines. This\nseems an extremely sensible guess, and one can check that if n = 5, we do get 16\nregions. So, having verified this formula for 5 points, are you satisfied that for any n\npoints there are 2n – 1 regions? If so, how would you respond, if someone asked you,\nhow you can be sure about this for n = 25, say? To deal with such questions, you\nwould need a proof which shows beyond doubt that this result is true, or a counter-\nexample to show that this result fails for some ‘n’. Actually, if you are patient and try\nit out for n = 6, you will find that there are 31 regions, and for n = 7 there are 57\nregions. So, n = 6, is a counter-example to the conjecture above. This demonstrates\nthe power of a counter-example. You may recall that in the Class IX we discussed\nthat to disprove a statement, it is enough to come up with a single counter-\nexample.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH1A1",
    "page": 7,
    "block_id": "jemh1a1_p007_b15",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.03,
      490.28,
      420.07,
      528.7
    ],
    "reading_order": 15,
    "text": "You may have noticed that we insisted on a proof regarding the number\nof regions in spite of verifying the result for n = 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5. Let us consider\na few more examples. You are familiar with the following result (given in Chapter 5):",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH1A1",
    "page": 7,
    "block_id": "jemh1a1_p007_b16",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.03,
      533.69,
      420.04,
      562.79
    ],
    "reading_order": 16,
    "text": "1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n = \n(\n1)\n2\nn n \n. To establish its validity, it is not enough to verify the",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH1A1",
    "page": 7,
